-- Paragraph (2) is asked first because paragraph (1) is "subject to paragraph (2)": its answer is passed in.
-- src:1033 | (2) A defendant to an action may not issue a third party notice without the permission of the Court unless the action was begun by originating claim and the defendant issues the third party notice before serving the defendant’s defence on the claimant.
@ref O 10 r 1(2) (src:1033)
GIVEN `the Court has given the defendant permission to issue a third party notice` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the action was begun by` IS A `Document kind`
      `the defendant issues the third party notice before serving the defendant's defence on the claimant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 10 r 1(2) the defendant may issue a third party notice` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the Court has given the defendant permission to issue a third party notice`) AND (NOT (`the action was begun by` EQUALS `Originating claim` AND `the defendant issues the third party notice before serving the defendant's defence on the claimant`))
    THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A defendant to an action may not issue a third party notice without the permission of the Court unless the action was begun by originating claim and the defendant issues the third party notice before serving the defendant's defence on the claimant" (flat negative, ruling N2)
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the prohibition is for a notice issued "without the permission of the Court" and outside the exception; with permission, or inside the exception, paragraph (2) is silent and paragraph (1) decides [marginal-b]

